# Cross-origin isolation for anything Cloudflare serves as a static asset,
# without the Worker running.
#
# The FSRS optimizer is a threaded wasm build, so it needs SharedArrayBuffer,
# which browsers only grant to a cross-origin isolated document. `require-corp`
# is the mode Safari supports.
#
# The document itself does NOT depend on this file: the Worker renders it, so
# Nitro's `routeRules` set both headers there (verified — a request to `/` comes
# back with `cross-origin-opener-policy: same-origin` and
# `cross-origin-embedder-policy: require-corp`). This file existed because SPA
# mode made the page a prerendered static asset that bypassed the Worker
# entirely; SPA mode is off now (see the note in vite.config.ts), so this is the
# belt to that braces — it keeps the guarantee if a page is ever served straight
# from the assets handler.
#
# Worth checking after a deploy either way: `crossOriginIsolated` in the console
# must be true, or auto-calibration silently declines to start and says so only
# in a console line (see zero/auto-calibration.ts).
#
# The cost of `require-corp` is that cross-origin subresources without CORP
# headers stop loading, which is why the Google avatar goes through /api/avatar
# rather than straight into an <img>.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
